// The ROAD DECK's sun-visibility BAKE, in TRACK space — one pass at track
// build, never per frame. The deck's half of what vvis.mat does for the ground.
//
// WHY THE DECK NEEDED ITS OWN MAP. The deck's light was baked PER VERTEX into
// custom0 (see fillRoadLight), on the premise that a shadow whose penumbra is
// 0.6 world units is smooth against the road's 0.48 u ring step. That premise
// holds ALONG the track and fails ACROSS it: the 16-point cross-section puts
// deck-level columns at lat ±half, ±(half-gap), ±(half-gap-lw) and ±dashW/2, so
// each lane spans ~2.15 u with no vertex in it at all. A cast shadow narrower
// than that — a barrel roll's, where the rolled deck turns edge-on to the sun
// and its shadow closes to ~2 u — lands between the columns and is interpolated
// into a 2.15 u linear ramp or lost outright. Measured on skyline's roll: the
// fully-dark core goes 1.62 u (truth) to 0.66 u (per vertex), and the per-vertex
// answer barely moves as the roll turns, because it is pinned to the columns
// rather than following the caster.
//
// WHY TRACK SPACE AND NOT THE LIGHT'S GRID. vvis bakes the ground on the light's
// own grid, which works because the ground is single-valued under a near-overhead
// sun. The road is not: a barrel roll sits directly over the deck it shadows and
// a loop has two arclengths at one (x, z), so both would land in the same texel.
// Track space is the one parameterisation where they are distinct — the same
// reason the deck's decals and its rubber layer live there.
//
// SO THE MESH IS RASTERIZED BY ITS OWN uv0. vertexDomain is `device`, and the
// bake mesh (buildRoadVisMesh) carries the deck's (s, lat) already mapped to
// clip space in POSITION, with the light-space uv and NoL riding custom0. Two
// consequences worth knowing:
//
//   - OFF-DECK STRIPS CLIP THEMSELVES OUT, for free. The road's section is a
//     CLOSED ring and only the strips with both endpoints at y == 0 carry real
//     track space; the kerbs, skirts and underside get OFF_DECK_LAT (1000), which
//     maps far outside the viewport. The deck flag is a per-QUAD constant, so
//     such a quad has all four corners outside and is dropped whole — no test
//     here, and no way for this pass and the decal loop to disagree about what
//     the deck is.
//   - AND THOSE STRIPS STILL GET A SHADOW, by CLAMP_TO_EDGE. The map's lat span
//     is the deck's own (±maxHalf), so a kerb's out-of-band lat clamps to the
//     outermost deck column beside it. A kerb top stands 0.26 u above the deck,
//     which displaces its true shadow by 0.26 × 0.208 = 0.05 u — far inside one
//     texel — so the deck's answer IS the kerb's. That is what keeps a shadow
//     band from stopping dead at the rail, with no second parameterisation.
//
// The visibility written here is the FULL runtime decode including the
// edge-on fade, so vroad multiplies it straight into its sun term.
material {
    name : TtpVroadvis,
    requires : [
        // (light-space uv .xyz, NoL) — see buildRoadVisMesh. NOT a world
        // position: the uv is affine in it and w is exactly 1 (ortho light), so
        // interpolating the uv is exact and keeps this varying in a range that
        // does not care about precision, where a world position would.
        custom0
    ],
    variables : [ probe ],
    vertexDomain : device,
    shadingModel : unlit,
    culling : none,
    depthWrite : false,
    depthCulling : false,
    parameters : [
        // The five shadow parameters, by the same names ttp_shade.inc reads
        // everywhere — bindShadowMap fills them on this instance too.
        { type : sampler2d, name : shadowMap, precision : high },
        { type : mat4, name : shadowFromWorld, precision : high },
        { type : float, name : shadowTexel },
        { type : float, name : shadowK },
        { type : float, name : shadowDepthScale }
    ]
}

vertex {
    void materialVertex(inout MaterialVertexInputs material) {
        material.probe = getCustom0();
    }
}

fragment {
    // SHADE_VERTEX drops the fwidth AA floor, exactly as vvis.mat's bake does:
    // that term guards SCREEN-space aliasing of the compare, and there is no
    // screen here. After the bake the map's own bilinear filtering plus the
    // world-unit floor on the ramp (kPenumbraWorld) carry that job.
    #define TTP_SHADE_VERTEX
    #include "ttp_shade.inc"

    void material(inout MaterialInputs material) {
        prepareMaterial(material);
        material.baseColor = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
        material.emissive = vec4(
                sunVisibilityUV(variable_probe.xyz, variable_probe.w),
                0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
    }
}
